<!doctype html>
<html lang="en"><meta charset="utf-8"><title>Getting started with jmpread</title>
<style>body{max-width:760px;margin:3em auto;font:18px/1.6 system-ui;padding:0 1em;color:#193242}pre{background:#f1f5f8;padding:1em;overflow:auto}h1,h2{line-height:1.2}</style>
<h1>Getting started with jmpread</h1>
<p>This package imports common JMP table encodings using native R. Python and JMP are not runtime dependencies. Compatibility is limited to the documented encodings.</p>
<h2>Read the bundled table</h2>
<!--begin.rcode setup
library(jmpread)
path <- system.file("extdata", "jmpreader_jl", "example1",
                    "example1.jmp", package = "jmpread")
data <- read_jmp(path)
data[, c("ints", "floats")]
end.rcode-->
<h2>Choose columns</h2>
<p>Column names and order follow the original table. select and exclude accept exact names.</p>
<!--begin.rcode select
read_jmp(path, select = c("floats", "ints"), metadata = FALSE)
data[["char utf8"]]
end.rcode-->
<h2>Keep dates and missing values</h2>
<p>JMP date and time displays store seconds since 1904-01-01. POSIXct preserves time of day even for date-looking values. Durations use difftime. Missing numeric values become NA; empty strings remain empty strings.</p>
<!--begin.rcode temporal
data$date
data$duration
read_jmp(path, select = "date", temporal = "raw", metadata = FALSE)
end.rcode-->
<h2>Inspect source metadata</h2>
<!--begin.rcode metadata
attr(data, "jmp_metadata")$build
attr(data[["date"]], "jmp_metadata")$storage
end.rcode-->
<p>Raw post-header blocks are retained, including payload bytes, so metadata can increase memory use. Notes, units, labels and formulas are not exposed as structured fields yet. Formulas are never executed. Row-state columns are lists of raw bytes.</p>
<h2>Use your own files</h2>
<p>Use a table you created yourself or have documented permission to use.</p>
<pre>my_table &lt;- read_jmp("my-data.jmp")
summary(my_table)</pre>
<h2>Help and support</h2>
<p>Use <code>?read_jmp</code> for all options and supported encodings. Report problems through the <a href="https://github.com/JosephHill04/jmpread/issues">issue tracker</a>, including your R version, package version and a small example you have permission to share.</p>
</html>
